# Results and Spatial Analysis

## Study Period
1948–1960

## Seagrass Area Statistics

 |       Metric          |      | Area (ha) |
 |Seagrass mapped in 1948|      |  6.9986   |
 |Seagrass mapped in 1960|      |  9.8356   |
 |Persistent mapped area |      |  3.9127   |
 |     Mapped loss       |      |  3.0858   |
 |  Mapped expansion     |      |  5.9229   |

## Spatial Persistence

- Approximately 55.9% of the 1948 mapped area overlapped with the 1960 mapped extent.
- Approximately 39.8% of the 1960 mapped area overlapped with the 1948 mapped extent.

These percentages describe spatial overlap between the two mapped survey periods. They do not establish continuous ecological survival between surveys.

## Interpretation

The mapped seagrass extent was larger in 1960 than in 1948. However, the spatial comparison also indicates substantial areas of mapped loss and expansion.

The results demonstrate changes in the distribution of mapped seagrass habitat across the study period.

## Limitations

- Historical survey methods and mapping accuracy may differ.
- Spatial overlap does not necessarily indicate continuous habitat survival.
- Mapped changes should not automatically be interpreted as definitive ecological gain or loss.
